  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Ratified in 1967, the 25th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution clarifies presidential succession and disability. It ensures a seamless transfer of power if the president dies, resigns, or becomes incapacitated. It allows the president to voluntarily transfer power to the Vice President, or for the VP and Cabinet to declare the president unfit. Key aspects of the 25th Amendment include: Section 1: Presidential Succession — Confirms that if the President dies, resigns, or is removed from office, the Vice President becomes President. Section 2: Vice Presidential Vacancy — Authorizes the President to nominate a new Vice President, subject to approval by a majority vote in both houses of Congress. Section 3: Voluntary Transfer of Power — Enables the President to declare in writing that they are unable to perform their duties, allowing the Vice President to become Acting President until the President declares they are able to return. Section 4: Involuntary Transfer of Power — Allows the Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet (or a body designated by Congress) to declare the President incapacitated by transmitting a written declaration to Congress. The Vice President immediately becomes Acting President. Challenging Section 4If the president declares that no inability exists, they can resume their powers. However, if the Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet contest this within four days, Congress must decide the issue, requiring a two-thirds vote in both houses to sustain the removal. The amendment was passed following the assassination of John F. Kennedy, which highlighted the need for clear rules on vice-presidential vacancies and presidential disability.",
